---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import MotionStage from '@/components/motion/MotionStage.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';
import MotionOptions from '@/components/motion/MotionOptions.astro';
import MotionTokens from '@/components/motion/MotionTokens.astro';
import Svg from '@/components/motion/scenes/Svg.astro';
import { SvgControls } from '@/components/motion/scenes/SvgControls';
import { MotionSvgReact } from '@/examples/react/motion-svg.tsx';
import { MotionSvgAngular } from '@/examples/angular/motion-svg.ts';
import MotionSvgSvelte from '@/examples/svelte/motion-svg.svelte';
import { chipClass } from '@/components/motionDemo';
---
<MotionPage
  slug="svg"
  title="Motion: draw or morph SVG"
  description="A stroke is drawn, a dot travels along a path, and one path morphs into another. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
>
  <MotionStage scene="svg" label="Live example: Draw or morph SVG" height={360}>
    <Fragment slot="controls"><SvgControls client:visible size="page" /></Fragment>
    <Svg size="page" />
    <Fragment slot="readout"><span class={`${chipClass} tracking-normal`} data-svg-readout>morph: 'Cookie'</span></Fragment>
  </MotionStage>

  <h2>How to use it</h2>
  <p>A motion binds on an SVG element as it does on an HTML one, with three exceptions: a layout motion is refused there, since it needs a box; a split plays the element whole, since its text is not cut; and values, sequences and gestures bind to HTML elements only. Two visual fields are for shapes and paths.</p>

  <h2>Draw</h2>
  <p><code>draw</code>, from 0 to 1, draws a path or a basic shape from nothing to its whole stroke. It sets <code>pathLength</code> to 1 and the dash of the stroke. A finished draw at 1 gives both back to the author at rest, and a binding gives them back when it is destroyed. The preset <code>draw</code> is an <code>appear</code> from nothing to the whole stroke.</p>
  <pre><code>{`<path data-motion="draw" d="M16 25L22 31L33 19"/>`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Along a path</h2>
  <p><code>along</code>, from 0 to 1, moves an element along the <code>offset-path</code> its CSS gives it, by writing <code>offset-distance</code>. An element that is on the page when the runtime connects keeps the <code>offset-distance</code> its CSS gives it: set <code>offset-distance: 100%</code> on a travel that should rest at its end.</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { defineMotion } from '@udixio/motion';

// The element travels from the start of its offset path to its end
defineMotion('travel', { verb: 'reveal', keyframes: [{ along: 0 }, { along: 1 }] });`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Morph</h2>
  <p><code>morph(path, to, &#123; timing, precision &#125;)</code>, from <code>@udixio/motion/svg</code>, morphs the <code>d</code> of a path into the <code>d</code> of another path element, or into a <code>d</code> string. Both shapes are resampled with anime.js, so they need not have the same number of points. The default timing is <code>spring.default.spatial</code>; a curve is its own easing. The path must be inside an SVG.</p>
  <pre><code>{`import { morph } from '@udixio/motion/svg';

const handle = morph(icon, 'M7 5 H10 V19 H7 Z M14 5 H17 V19 H14 Z');
await handle.finished; // 'finished' once the shape is there
handle.cancel();       // leaves the shape where it is`}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>precision</code> is the number of points per unit of length the shapes are sampled at; 0 morphs the two strings as they are.</li>
    <li>The handle ends when the shape is there, and the path then carries exactly the <code>d</code> of the target.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use it in the frameworks</h2>
  <p>
    The check is a reveal: it is drawn once, when it enters the view, and <em>Draw again</em> draws it from nothing. The dot
    is a reveal along its path. The icon morphs between play and pause, and a new morph continues from the shape the icon has reached.
    React <code>useMotion('check-draw')</code> with <code>play()</code>, Angular <code>udxMotion="check-draw"</code>, Svelte
    <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion('check-draw')&#125;</code>.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-svg" previewHeight={[480, 456]}>
    <MotionSvgReact slot="react" client:visible />
    <MotionSvgAngular slot="angular" client:visible />
    <MotionSvgSvelte slot="svelte" client:visible />
  </MotionUsage>

  <h2>Options</h2>
  <MotionOptions
    rows={[
      { name: 'draw', values: 'keyframes from 0 to 1', description: 'Draws a path or a basic shape from nothing to its whole stroke.' },
      { name: 'along', values: 'keyframes from 0 to 1', description: 'Moves the element along the offset path its CSS gives it.' },
      { name: 'timing', values: 'spring.* token, or a curve', default: 'spring.default.spatial', description: 'The timing of a morph. A spring falls back to its curve on anime.js.' },
      { name: 'precision', values: 'a number of points per unit', description: 'How finely the two shapes are resampled. 0 morphs the strings as they are.' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Timing and tokens</h2>
  <p>A morph moves on the default spatial spring. The check and the dot are drawn over the extra long curve.</p>
  <MotionTokens tokens={['spring.default.spatial', 'duration.extraLong1', 'duration.extraLong2', 'easing.standard']} />

  <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An entry with <code>draw</code> sets its stroke at once, and an <code>along</code> entry moves at once.</li>
    <li>An exit keeps the stroke it has, or the place it is at, and fades: nothing is redrawn or moved under reduced motion.</li>
    <li><code>morph</code> sets the shape at once under reduced motion, with <code>--udx-motion: none</code>, or with a zero motion scale.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A layout motion is refused on an SVG element.</li>
    <li>Two motions that draw one shape share its <code>pathLength</code>: it stays 1 while either draws.</li>
    <li><code>morph</code> needs anime.js, which the consumer installs alongside the package.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
